| Step                                            | What to do                                                                                                                                                                                          | What you are checking for                                                                                     |
| ----------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 1. Check the power adapter and outlet           | Connect the charger firmly and test a different wall outlet. Check whether the charger’s indicator light is on.                                                                                     | Determines whether the problem is caused by a dead outlet, damaged charger, or loose connection.              |
| 2. Inspect the charging port                    | Check the laptop’s DC-in/USB-C charging port for dust, bent pins, looseness, or physical damage.                                                                                                    | Checks whether the laptop is actually receiving power from the charger.                                       |
| 3. Perform a hard power reset                   | Disconnect the charger, remove the removable battery if possible, hold the power button for about 15–30 seconds, reconnect power, and try starting it.                                              | Clears residual electrical charge and can recover a laptop that is stuck in a power-management state.         |
| 4. Check battery condition                      | Try powering the laptop using the charger with the battery removed, when the model allows it. Check for battery swelling or other visible damage.                                                   | Determines whether a failed or damaged battery is preventing startup.                                         |
| 5. Check internal hardware/service indicators   | Look for charging LEDs, keyboard lights, fan movement, beep codes, or diagnostic LED blink patterns. If there is still no response, have the motherboard and power circuitry professionally tested. | Helps determine whether the problem is a motherboard, power circuit, RAM, or other internal hardware failure. |
